# Playwright Conventions

Examples are copied from this repo. If a ✅ or ❌ is missing, that pattern is not in the repo.

## 1. Locators

Use `getByRole`, then `getByLabel` / `getByPlaceholder`, then `getByText`, and disambiguate with `.filter({ hasText })`.

✅ Good — `pages/ProgramsPage.ts`

```typescript
this.newProgramButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: '+ New Program' });
this.emptyStateMessage = page.getByRole('paragraph').filter({
  hasText: 'No programs yet. Create your first program to get started.',
});
```

✅ Good — `pages/NewProgramModal.ts`

```typescript
this.programNameInput = this.dialog.getByLabel('Program Name');
```

❌ Bad — no CSS or XPath locator in this repo.

Why: a role or label survives markup changes that break a class or XPath.

## 2. Waiting

Wait with a web-first `expect` or for the response that unlocks the next assertion. Do not use `waitForTimeout`.

✅ Good — `tests/programs-api-edge-cases.spec.ts`

```typescript
await modal.clickCreate();
const response = await failedSave;
await expect(modal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

`failedSave` is `page.waitForResponse(...)` for `POST /api/programs` with status 503.

❌ Bad — no `waitForTimeout` in this repo.

Why: a fixed sleep passes or fails on machine speed instead of the UI reaching the expected state.

## 3. Page Objects

Keep locators and actions on the page object; keep every `expect` in the spec.

✅ Good — `tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`

```typescript
const programsPage = new ProgramsPage(page);
await programsPage.goto();
await programsPage.tabToNewProgramButton();
await expect(programsPage.newProgramButton).toBeFocused();
await programsPage.pressEnter();
await expect(modal.dialog).toBeVisible();
```

`tabToNewProgramButton` and `pressEnter` in `pages/ProgramsPage.ts` perform the keys and do not assert.

❌ Bad — no spec in this repo asserts inside a page object or inlines a locator.

Why: an assertion buried in the page object hides which behavior the test owns and is easy to skip.

## 4. Auth

Feature tests reuse the storage state written once by the setup project.

✅ Good — `playwright.config.ts`

```typescript
{
  name: "setup",
  testMatch: /.*\.setup\.ts/,
},
{
  name: "chromium",
  use: {
    ...devices["Desktop Chrome"],
    storageState: path.join(__dirname, "playwright/.auth/user.json"),
  },
  dependencies: ["setup"],
},
```

✅ Good — `tests/auth.setup.ts` signs in with `getByLabel` / `getByRole` and saves that file:

```typescript
await page.context().storageState({ path: authFile });
```

❌ Bad — no feature spec in this repo logs in through the UI on every test.

Why: repeating login makes every test depend on the login page and burns time before the assertion.

## 5. Test data

Track only the program id this test created, and let the fixture delete it.

✅ Good — `pages/ProgramsPage.ts`

```typescript
const id = await modal.submitCreate();
options?.trackProgram?.(id);
```

✅ Good — `tests/programs-api-edge-cases.spec.ts` tracks an id only when the mocked create unexpectedly succeeds:

```typescript
if (response.ok()) {
  const body = await response.json();
  const id = body?.data?.id;
  if (typeof id === 'string') trackProgram(id);
}
```

❌ Bad — no spec deletes a program it did not create, and none hardcodes `DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN`.

Why: leftover rows pile up and a later test collides with data it does not own.

## 6. API testing

Call the API with the cleanup token and delete only a tracked id.

✅ Good — `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts`

```typescript
return await api.delete(`/api/programs/${id}`);
```

The same fixture sends `Authorization: Bearer ${token}` from `tokenForCleanup()`, refreshes once on 401, and throws if the delete is not ok.

❌ Bad — no spec in this repo mocks `/api/programs` and then asserts that the real API returned a given status.

Why: a mock in front of the call you are verifying can only prove the mock, not the API.

## 7. Network mocking

Mock only the programs response whose UI reaction you are testing, and `route.continue()` every other request.

✅ Good — `tests/programs-api-edge-cases.spec.ts`

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/programs**', async (route) => {
  if (route.request().method() === method && isProgramsCollection(route)) {
    return route.fulfill({
      status,
      contentType: 'application/json',
      body,
    });
  }
  return route.continue();
});
```

The empty-list test fulfills `GET` 200 with `JSON.stringify({ data: [] })`. A root array `[]` is not what that test uses.

❌ Bad — no spec aborts `**/*` or mocks a call and treats that mock as the API contract.

Why: a broad abort or a mock of the asserted endpoint makes the test green without the app or the API doing the work.

## 8. Accessibility

Scan the page with WCAG 2 A and AA tags, assert `results.violations` is empty locally, and cover keyboard focus separately. On CI, record violations and do not fail the job.

✅ Good — `tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`

```typescript
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa']).analyze();
const summary = await recordAxeViolations(results);
if (process.env.CI) {
  if (results.violations.length > 0) {
    console.warn(`[a11y advisory — CI does not fail on axe violations]\n${summary}`);
  }
  return;
}
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
```

The same file tabs to the New Program button, asserts `toBeFocused()`, presses Enter, and asserts the dialog is visible. The page scan does not use `.include()` or `.exclude()`.

❌ Bad — `tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts` still disables a real rule on the modal scan:

```typescript
.disableRules(['button-name'])
```

Why: turning a rule off, or skipping the keyboard path, hides a failure axe or a pointer click would never show.

## 9. Assertions

Assert the locator with a retrying matcher, and give each test exactly one tag.

✅ Good — `tests/programs-api-edge-cases.spec.ts`

```typescript
test(
  'Empty program list shows the empty state',
  { tag: '@regression' },
  async ({ page }) => {
    await expect(programsPage.emptyStateMessage).toBeVisible();
    await expect(programsPage.emptyStateCreateButton).toBeVisible();
  },
);
```

Also in that spec: `toHaveValue(programName)` and `toHaveCount(0)`.

❌ Bad — no `expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true)` in this repo.

Why: a one-shot boolean does not retry, so a slow paint fails a check that would have passed a moment later.

## 10. Anti-flake

Run fully parallel, retry twice on CI only, trace the first retry, and pin `en-CA` / `America/Toronto`. Do not set `workers: 1`.

✅ Good — `playwright.config.ts`

```typescript
fullyParallel: true,
retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
use: {
  baseURL: process.env.DIDAXIS_URL,
  locale: "en-CA",
  timezoneId: "America/Toronto",
  trace: "on-first-retry",
},
```

❌ Bad — no `page.clock.install()` in this repo yet, and `workers` is not set.

Why: an unpinned timezone or a single worker makes dates and failures differ between your machine and CI, and extra retries hide a local bug.

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| `getByRole` / `getByLabel`, then `.filter({ hasText })` | CSS or XPath locators |
| `expect(locator).toBeVisible()` / `toBeFocused()` / `toHaveCount()` | `waitForTimeout` or `expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true)` |
| Locators and actions in `pages/`, `expect` in the spec | Inline locators or assertions inside the page object |
| Setup project `storageState` for feature tests | Log in through the UI in every feature test |
| `trackProgram(id)` and `DELETE /api/programs/<uuid>` with the bearer token | Delete rows the test did not create, or hardcode the token |
| Mock only the UI reaction under test; `route.continue()` the rest | Mock the endpoint whose real contract you are asserting |
| `withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])` and a keyboard `toBeFocused()` check | `.disableRules(...)` to force a green scan |
| One tag per test; `retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0` | `workers: 1`, or retries above 2 |
| `locale: "en-CA"` and `timezoneId: "America/Toronto"` | Leave date formatting on the machine timezone |
